{"id": "81bee4fe-e000-405f-ba31-7c5c2099d961", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:37:04.761805", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0e926453-b65e-474a-93f4-0c72d83c552d", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The workspace is essentially empty: only task.json. No track.md file present, no repository, no installed packages, no network. So I'll base the design on knowledge of the JaxMARL and SMAX codebases (public knowledge) and clearly flag that the local environment has no sources beyond task.json.\n\nGiven \"design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\", the design should be grounded in what is known about JaxMARL and SMAX APIs from the literature/source, and I should state which specifics are source-supported vs. assumed.\n\nLet me recall what I know about JaxMARL:\n\nJaxMARL (Rutherford et al., 2023, NeurIPS) — a JAX-based MARL library with:\n- Envs: MPE, Overcooked, Hanabi, RWARE, SMAC (SMACv1/StarCraft Multi-Agent Challenge), and SMAX (a JAX re-implementation of SMAC).\n- Algorithms implemented: PPO, IQL (Independent Q-Learning), QMIX? Let me recall. JaxMARL ships with baselines: PPO, IQL (IL / independent Q-learning), and... I believe they include `ippo`, `iql`, `qmix`? Actually the JaxMARL repo has algorithms folder with `ppo`, `iql`, `qmix`? Let me recall. In the original JaxMARL release (v1.0), algorithms included: PPO and IQL. QMIX was added later? Let me think.\n\nActually the original JaxMARL repo (jaxmarl/jaxmarl) includes:\n- `jaxmarl/algorithms/ppo/` — PPO (with recurrent variant? there's `ppo` and `rware`... )\n- `jaxmarl/algorithms/iql/` — IQL (independent Q-learning, with value-based training)\n- `jaxmarl/algorithms/qmix/` — QMIX was added in a later release (v1.1?).\n\nHmm, I recall the paper \"JaxMARL: Multi-Agent RL Environments in JAX\" describes implementing PPO and IQL baselines, and mentions that they also ported QMIX? Let me recall the README: \"Available algorithms: PPO, IPPO, IQL, QMIX, MAPPO...\" Actually.\n\nLet me think carefully. The JaxMARL GitHub README lists:\n- Environments: Cleaner, Coin Game, Hanabi, Level-Based Foraging (LBF), MPE, Overcooked, RWARE, SMAX, SMAC, Stochastic, Multiwalker, ...\n\nAnd \"Baselines\": \"We provide implementations of PPO, IQL, QMIX ...\" Hmm.\n\nI recall the repo has `jaxmarl/algorithms/` with subfolders: `ppo` (ppov2), `iql`, `qmix`, `maddpg`? Let me recall. Actually I think they have: `jaxmarl/algorithms/ppo/` (with `ppo` and `recurrent` variants), `jaxmarl/algorithms/iql/`, `jaxmarl/algorithms/qmix/`, `jaxmarl/algorithms/tremarl/`, `jaxmarl/algorithms/maddpg/`? Not sure about maddpg.\n\nI should be careful: I shouldn't over-claim specifics I'm unsure about. I'll structure the plan with known-stable facts (PPO and IQL are definitely in JaxMARL as the paper's main baselines) and flag QMIX as the value-decomposition option that may need verification against the source.\n\nActually I'm fairly confident about the following from the JaxMARL repo:\n- `jaxmarl/algorithms/ppo/ppov2.py` — a PPO implementation using flax.\n- IPPO is the multi-agent application of PPO (each agent independent but centralized training? IPPO = independent PPO, meaning each agent has its own network).\n- `jaxmarl/algorithms/iql/iql.py` — independent Q-learning.\n- `jaxmarl/algorithms/qmix/qmix.py` — QMIX implementation.\n\nI recall that JaxMARL added QMIX and also the \"SMAC benchmark\" results. The paper's experiments compare PPO (IPPO), IQL, and QMIX on several environments including SMAX.\n\nSMAX: \"SMAX: A Simplified Version of SMAC\" by Rutherford et al. It's a vectorized, JAX-based environment that mimics the StarCraft II SMAC maps but simplified. SMAX has maps like: `3m`, `8m`, `25m`, `5m_vs_6m`, `MMM2`, `corridor`, `2s3z`, `8m_vs_9m`, `3s5z_vs_3s6z`, `10m_vs_11m`, `27m_vs_30m`, `bane_vs_bane`, `2c_vs_64zg`, `protoss_units`, `zerg_units`, `terran_units`, ... SMAX provides a set of 8 \"SMAX maps\" (some subset) with heuristics-based bots. The `SMAX` wrapper maps to a set of maps: I recall `get_smax_maps()` returns ~12 maps. Default map is \"3m\".\n\nSMAX observation: each agent observes relative positions, health, shields, cooldowns of nearby units (like SMAC but simplified, no terrain). Actions: move 4 dirs, stop, attack 1..k enemies, no-op? Actually \"attack_enemies\" is a single action that auto-attacks nearest enemy. So action space: move_N, move_S, move_E, move_W, stop, attack, no-op. 7 actions.\n\nSMAX uses a bot for the enemy: a heuristic bot.\n\nEvaluation metric: win rate vs the built-in heuristic bot. SMAX reports win rate over evaluation episodes. The paper uses \"win rate\" and \"mean episode return\". Normalized score for the comparison could be win rate.\n\nLet me recall SMAX maps list from source (`smax.py` in `jaxmarl/environments/smax.py`): The `MAP_NAMES` list includes:\n- \"3m\"\n- \"8m\"\n- \"25m\"\n- \"5m_vs_6m\"\n- \"8m_vs_9m\"\n- \"10m_vs_11m\"\n- \"27m_vs_30m\"\n- \"MMM2\"\n- \"corridor\"\n- \"3s5z\"\n- \"3s5z_vs_3s6z\"\n- \"2s3z\"\n- \"2c_vs_64zg\"\n- \"bane_vs_bane\"\n\nSomething like that. There might be 4 maps in the SMAX benchmark (3m, 8m, 25m, 5m_vs_6m, MMM2, corridor, 2c_vs_64zg). Not 100% sure.\n\nI should flag uncertainties.\n\nNow the question: \"How does JaxMARL's PPO compare to Q-Learning in SMAX environments in terms of training time and normalized score?\"\n\nThis suggests a comparison of:\n- PPO: JaxMARL's IPPO implementation (the PPO baseline in JaxMARL for MARL).\n- Q-Learning: JaxMARL's IQL implementation (Independent Q-Learning) and possibly QMIX (value decomposition). The question says \"Q-Learning\" generally — IQL is the natural choice; QMIX as a value-decomposition variant would strengthen the comparison.\n\nMetrics:\n- Training time: wall-clock per environment step (throughput) and total wall-clock to reach a target. Since GPU unavailable, CPU-only with stated parallelism assumptions.\n- Normalized score: win rate against built-in SMAX bot, normalized by reference (e.g., random policy baseline and/or a reference algorithm's final performance; or normalized so that 0 = random policy, 1 = bot win rate? Or per-map best-known). Need to define precisely.\n\nLet me design:\n\nFixed requirements (from the task):\n- Compare JaxMARL PPO vs Q-Learning in SMAX.\n- Measure training time and normalized score.\n- Design-only; no experiment run; must not claim a completed experiment.\n- CPU-only environment (GPU unavailable), 224 CPU affinity count hint.\n\nOpen choices:\n- Which specific Q-learning variant (IQL vs QMIX vs both).\n- Which SMAX maps.\n- Definition of \"training time\" metric.\n- Definition of \"normalized score\".\n- Hyperparameters, budget, seeds, evaluation protocol.\n\nApproach:\n\nPhase 0 — setup/verification (design-only grounding):\n- Note that JaxMARL is installable via pip or from source; SMAX is bundled in JaxMARL (not a separate repo). Actually SMAX is included in jaxmarl.environments.smax. In later JaxMARL versions, SMAX also exists as a separate installable `smax` package? I believe the standalone package \"SMAX\" was split out in v1.1 or so: `jaxmarl/environments/smax.py` and SMAX available via `pip install smax`? Hmm. I recall SMAX became an independent package (github.com/flowersteam/SMAX) with pip install. But JaxMARL bundles it. Flag as uncertainty.\n\nKey design points:\n\n1. Algorithms\n- PPO: JaxMARL's IPPO (PPO with shared or per-agent networks, clipped surrogate loss, GAE, minibatch updates, flax). Use default hyperparameters from JaxMARL's config for SMAX (which the repo tunes).\n- Q-Learning: IQL (Independent Q-Learning) from JaxMARL — DQN-style with replay buffer, target network, epsilon-greedy. Optionally QMIX for value decomposition. Given the question says \"Q-Learning\", IQL is the primary; QMIX is a secondary to test whether value-decomposition changes the conclusion. \n\n2. Environments / scenarios\n- Choose a small set spanning difficulty: \"3m\" (easy symmetric), \"8m\", \"5m_vs_6m\" (asymmetric), \"MMM2\" or \"2c_vs_64zg\" (hard). Or follow the SMAX benchmark maps. Keep 3 maps for tractability: easy (3m), medium (8m), hard-asymmetric (5m_vs_6m).\n\n3. Training time measurement\n- No GPU: use CPU. Report:\n  - Throughput: environment steps per second (or per wall-clock hour) during training, measured as total env steps / wall-clock.\n  - Total wall-clock to reach a fixed step budget (or to reach target normalized score).\n  - Note: PPO is off-policy-independent (on-policy), uses vectorized rollouts; IQL needs replay buffers and off-policy updates; QMIX centralizes. Wall-clock comparison must fix the same number of environment steps and same env parallelism (e.g., 128 or 256 parallel envs via JAX vmap; on CPU with 224 cores).\n  - Since timing is machine-dependent, run timing on same machine, median over seeds, and report steps/sec rather than raw wall-clock for cross-machine comparability.\n\n4. Normalized score\n- Primary: win rate vs built-in SMAX heuristic bot over N evaluation episodes (e.g., 100 episodes, deterministic at eval, e.g., greedy for Q-learning, deterministic actions/mean for PPO).\n- Normalized score: define as (win_rate - win_rate_random) / (win_rate_reference - win_rate_random), where random = uniform policy win rate (measured), reference = built-in bot win rate vs itself or a strong baseline (e.g., 1.0 by definition if we treat bot-vs-bot as reference, or the best known per map). Alternatively use SMAC-style \"battle win rate\" directly and normalize to [0,1].\n- Simpler, robust: normalized score = win rate itself scaled to [0,1]; plus secondary metric = battle win rate per map. Provide definition precisely.\n\nActually the term \"normalized score\" in the question — need to define. I'll propose: normalize win rate by a per-map reference such that random = 0 and built-in bot (used as opponent) = 1, i.e., relative to the heuristic bot. This ties normalized score to the bot's own strength per map.\n\n5. Controls\n- Same total environment steps budget (e.g., 2M env steps per map-seed, or map-appropriate budget: 3m/8m 2M, 16M for larger maps).\n- Same number of parallel environments (e.g., 256) for rollouts where applicable (both should use env-vectorized sampling to control parallelism; Q-learning may use fewer; must fix).\n- Same network architecture: MLP with same hidden sizes and activation (e.g., [128,128] tanh or ReLU) — but note IQL outputs Q values (one head per action), PPO outputs policy+value; keep parameter counts comparable where reasonable.\n- Same observation/action encoding (SMAX wrapper defaults).\n- Same learning rate and optimizer where applicable; report JaxMARL default configs and keep them fixed; hyperparameters per algorithm tuned equal-ish budget (report both \"defaults\" and \"tuned\" if budget allows).\n- Evaluation protocol: evaluate every K updates (e.g., every 10k env steps), N=100 eval episodes, no exploration.\n- Seeds: e.g., 10 seeds for small maps, 5 for large.\n- Same RNG key management.\n- Same discount, reward normalization, max episode length (SMAX default).\n- Timing on identical machine, same core count (pin to a fixed number of cores, e.g., 64 or 128 of the 224), median of ≥3 runs for timing.\n\n6. Analysis plan\n- Report per map: training curve (score vs env steps) and score vs wall-clock (cumulative time), mean±SEM across seeds.\n- Compute final normalized score at budget; compare via paired (per-seed) differences; report effect sizes and bootstrap CIs; use a threshold (e.g., ≥0.05 in normalized score or non-overlapping CIs of at least X) for \"meaningful\".\n- Time-to-target analysis: for each run, the wall-clock to first achieve target win rate (e.g., bot-vs-bot threshold or 0.5); compare distributions across seeds (Mann-Whitney or bootstrap CI of median).\n- Tradeoff: produce Pareto-style summary (score vs wall-clock) and compute \"score per unit time\" efficiency metric; identify regimes where each wins.\n\n7. Feasibility\n- Design-only; but note compute estimate: 224 CPU cores, JAX CPU vectorization; PPO with 256 envs across 2M steps = ~8k rollout calls; IQL similar. Provide rough runtime estimates as feasibility note, with the caveat that exact numbers come from the run.\n- Actually as design-only, \"feasible checks\" means things like: verify SMAX map list and bot via source inspection at run time; verify IQL/PPO config keys; measure random-policy win rate as baseline.\n\nNow, I need to distinguish source-supported facts from assumptions. Since we have no source files locally, I must clearly state that all JaxMARL/SMAX API specifics are from knowledge of the public codebase and must be verified at run time (or against a pinned version), flagging things like:\n- Exact map list in `get_smax_maps()`.\n- Whether QMIX is available in the installed JaxMARL version.\n- Whether SMAX is bundled or separate package.\n- Default eval protocol and bot implementation details.\n\nLet me also recall the setup notes referenced: \"neurips2024/97649/0:setup_2\", \"setup_4\", \"setup_12\" — these are topic IDs citing JaxMARL and SMAX setup notes. They aren't present locally. I'll note that these referenced setup notes are not available in the workspace, so source-level grounding must rely on the public JaxMARL/SMAX codebases and pinning a version.\n\nLet me verify a couple of things about SMAX from memory and maybe try pip to see if network is off (it is). Let me check pip download would fail due to no network. Not necessary.\n\nLet me also double check: is there any conda/pip cache? Not needed.\n\nLet me quickly check for any pip index or offline wheels. Not worth it.\n\nNow, \"Question budget: 6, continue_after_initial_plan: false\". The researcher asked for initial inspection only and a plan proposal (stage=plan). Good.\n\nI should keep the planning message concise but complete: \n1) What's available.\n2) Proposed core design choices with rationale.\n3) Controls and analysis plan.\n4) Assumptions/unknowns vs fixed requirements.\n\nLet me also think about what \"training time\" precisely: The question says \"in terms of training time and normalized score\". So we compare PPO vs Q-learning on (a) training time (wall-clock), (b) normalized score (performance). \n\nDefinition of training time:\n- I'll propose: total wall-clock measured on a fixed CPU machine with pinned core count, reported as (i) throughput in env-steps/sec, (ii) cumulative wall-clock at each checkpoint, and (iii) time-to-target (wall-clock to reach a target normalized score). This handles the \"training time\" cleanly.\n- Also note steps may differ in meaning: PPO on-policy does 1 update per N rollout steps; IQL does updates interleaved; better to report wall-clock at fixed env-step checkpoints, and separately wall-clock per unit env step.\n\nNormalized score:\n- Define per-map: ns = (wr − wr_rand)/(wr_ref − wr_rand), where wr = eval win rate, wr_rand = random-policy win rate (measured in-silico), wr_ref = heuristic bot's own win rate (bot-as-allied vs bot-as-enemy, measured) or 1.0. Provide fallback: if bot-vs-bot = 1.0, then normalized score reduces to win rate vs random baseline.\n- Alternatively, report both raw win rate and normalized score.\n\nWhich Q-learning: IQL primary (most faithful to \"Q-Learning\" in MARL and what JaxMARL ships); QMIX as a secondary value-decomposition baseline if available in the pinned version — flagging as optional/unknown.\n\nI think for the plan, propose: primary comparison IPPO vs IQL across 3 SMAX maps; optionally add QMIX. Good.\n\nMaps: propose 3m (easy), 8m (medium), 5m_vs_6m (medium asymmetric/interesting). Maybe add 2c_vs_64zg as hard — but tractability on CPU; keep 3.\n\nTraining budget: 2M env steps for 3m/8m, maybe 4-6M for 5m_vs_6m. Actually asymmetric maps", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "ls -la /tmp /home/actor 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; find / -maxdepth 3 -name '*.md' -o -maxdepth 3 -name '*.txt' 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E 'usr|etc|proc|sys|lib|boot' | head; echo '---'; nproc; cat /proc/cpuinfo | grep -c processor; free -g | head -2", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a425b67fed0349108b3be6c0b1fee360", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a425b67fed0349108b3be6c0b1fee360", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"ls -la /tmp /home/actor 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; find / -maxdepth 3 -name '*.md' -o -maxdepth 3 -name '*.txt' 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E 'usr|etc|proc|sys|lib|boot' | head; echo '---'; nproc; cat /proc/cpuinfo | grep -c processor; free -g | head -2\", \"summary\": \"Check for any docs, CPU count, and memory\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-24bbe6eced394b969617482100160eb0", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Check for any docs, CPU count, and memory", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
