{"id": "b508b7bf-bda6-4f0c-9c37-b48e168d0dea", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:37:29.491724", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "09e059cc-a81d-402b-b336-32bca6e7ddf8", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "I've confirmed the environment. Now I'll prepare the plan proposal.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "Environment confirmed: 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, no packages, no network, no docs beyond task.json.\n\nNow I have enough to propose the plan. Let me write the stage=plan handoff.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "Proposed approach (design only; no experiment will be run):\n\nA. Scope of the comparison\n- Algorithms: JaxMARL's own implementations, per the public codebase (pin a version, e.g., the released JaxMARL tag matching SMAX):\n  - PPO side = IPPO (independent PPO: per-agent policy+value MLPs, GAE, clipped surrogate), using JaxMARL's ppov2 config tuned for SMAX.\n  - Q-Learning side = IQL (independent DQN-style Q-learning with replay buffer, target network, epsilon-greedy) from JaxMARL's iql baseline. QMIX (value decomposition) added as a secondary arm ONLY if the pinned JaxMARL version includes it (must be verified against source at run time; flagged as unknown below).\n- Scenarios: a small, difficulty-spanned subset of SMAX maps: \"3m\" (easy), \"8m\" (medium), \"5m_vs_6m\" (asymmetric). Rationale: SMAX's heuristic-bot opponent and small action space make all maps feasible on CPU; 3 maps keep compute tractable while covering symmetric/asymmetric and easy/hard regimes.\n\nB. Training-time definition (no GPU; CPU-only with 224 cores visible)\n- Fix environment parallelism (e.g., 256 parallel envs via JAX vmap, pinned to a stated number of cores, e.g., 64, on the same machine) for BOTH algorithms so env-step throughput is comparable.\n- Report three quantities: (1) throughput = env-steps/s during training (stable after warmup), (2) cumulative wall-clock at fixed env-step checkpoints (so curves can be plotted vs time), (3) time-to-target = wall-clock to first reach a target normalized score. All timing taken on the identical machine with core count pinned, median over ≥3 repeated timing runs.\n\nC. Normalized-score definition\n- Primary per-map metric: normalized score = (wr − wr_rand)/(wr_ref − wr_rand), where wr = eval win rate vs the built-in SMAX bot (greedy/deterministic eval, N≥100 episodes), wr_rand = random-policy win rate measured in-silico, wr_ref = reference win rate (heuristic bot vs itself, measured in-silico; if bot-vs-bot ≈ 1, this reduces to win rate rescaled by the random baseline). Rationale: normalizes away map difficulty so scores are comparable across maps.\n- Also report raw win rate and mean episode return as secondary metrics. Bot details (its decision rule) verified from smax source at run time.\n\nD. Controls for fairness\n- Fixed: total env-step budget per map-seed (e.g., 2M for 3m/8m, 4M for 5m_vs_6m); identical network capacity (MLP [128,128], same activation, same observation/action encoding from the SMAX wrapper); same discount, max episode length, reward shaping (SMAX defaults); same evaluation cadence and episode count; hyperparameters set from each algorithm's JaxMARL default config and reported verbatim (no per-algorithm tuning advantage; tuning study optional/secondary); seeds ≥10 (small maps) / 5 (bigger); identical RNG management; timing runs repeated ≥3×.\n\nE. Analysis plan\n- Per map: mean±SEM curves of (a) normalized score vs env steps and (b) normalized score vs wall-clock time, across seeds.\n- Final-score comparison via paired per-seed differences with bootstrap CIs and effect sizes; \"meaningful difference\" threshold stated a priori (e.g., |Δ| ≥ 0.05 normalized score AND non-overlapping bootstrap CIs, or a stated effect size).\n- Time-to-target distributions across seeds compared via bootstrap CI of the median (Mann-Whitney as a robustness check).\n- Tradeoff: score vs wall-clock overlay plus a \"score per unit time\" efficiency index to make the speed/quality tradeoff explicit.\n\nF. Feasibility checks (in-design, not claims)\n- Version-pin JaxMARL; inspect source (get_smax_maps map list, smax bot, iql/ppo config keys, qmix availability) before any run.\n- Sanity checks: random-policy win rate → 0 baseline; bot-vs-bot → 1 baseline; throughput measured on 3m before committing to full budget.\n\nKey assumption/unknown notes are in the assumptions/uncertainties fields.", "evidence": "Inspection results:\n- /workspace contains only task.json; no repository, no track.md file, no datasets, no pre-installed packages.\n- Network is disabled and none of jax, jaxmarl, or smax are installed (import checks all fail).\n- System: 224 CPUs (nproc), ~2 TB RAM, no GPU (GPU_enabled=false), software env = system Python only.\n- The referenced setup topics (neurips2024/97649/0:setup_2/4/12 citing JaxMARL and SMAX) are not present as local files; they exist only as topic references, so source-level grounding must rely on the public JaxMARL/SMAX codebases pinned by version.\n- The machine/CPU facts (224 cores, no GPU, memory) are observed and support the CPU-only wall-clock design.", "assumptions": ["JaxMARL can be installed (pip/from source) at design-run time via its pinned tag; APIs are as in the public repo: IPPO in jaxmarl/algorithms/ppo, IQL in jaxmarl/algorithms/iql, SMAX bundled under jaxmarl/environments/smax with a heuristic bot and win-rate eval harness.", "SMAX map names (3m, 8m, 5m_vs_6m) and the bot-vs-self win rate ≈ 1 are as in the public SMAX implementation; exact availability must be confirmed from source at run time.", "QMIX availability in the installed JaxMARL version is unknown; it is an optional secondary arm only.", "Wall-clock timing is machine-dependent; design keeps it comparable by fixing machine, core pinning, and parallelism for both algorithms, and reporting throughput + time-to-target rather than a single absolute number.", "Default hyperparameters in the