{"id": "8c9818bb-3535-48cd-837c-bb8d4042fe4b", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:37:47.927283", "source": "user", "parent_id": "7ec8b9a5-59ef-4663-8af5-72025ee8f224", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"I approve the proposed approach. I am accepting the following as the agreed working basis, incorporating the fixed study requirement (compare PPO and Q-Learning for MARL in SMAX on training efficiency and performance, using JaxMARL and SMAX as context):\\n\\n1. Scope: PPO side = IPPO (JaxMARL's ppo implementation: per-agent policy+value MLPs, GAE, clipped surrogate); Q-Learning side = IQL (independent DQN-style with replay buffer, target network, epsilon-greedy) from JaxMARL's iql baseline. QMIX (value decomposition) is an optional secondary arm to be included ONLY if the pinned JaxMARL version ships it, with its availability stated explicitly. Scenarios: 3m, 8m, 5m_vs_6m, justified by symmetric/asymmetric and easy/hard coverage and CPU tractability.\\n2. Timing: CPU-only (no GPU), fixed parallelism (e.g., 256 envs via JAX vmap on a stated pinned core count), reporting throughput (env-steps/s after warmup), cumulative wall-clock at fixed env-step checkpoints, and time-to-target; median of >=3 repeated timing runs; no single absolute number claimed.\\n3. Normalized score: (wr - wr_rand)/(wr_ref - wr_rand) with wr_ref ~ 1 (bot-vs-self), raw win rate and mean episode return as secondary metrics; this is OUR metric definition (task does not pre-specify it) and must be labeled as such, with the bot rule verified from source.\\n4. Controls: fixed env-step budgets per map-seed (2M/4M), identical MLP [128,128] capacity and observation/action encoding, same discount/max-episode-length/reward shaping (SMAX defaults), eval cadence and episode count, hyperparameters taken verbatim from each algorithm's JaxMARL default config, seeds >=10 (small) / 5 (bigger), identical RNG and core pinning.\\n5. Analysis: mean±SEM curves of normalized score vs env steps and vs wall-clock; paired per-seed differences with bootstrap CIs and a priori meaningful-difference threshold; time-to-target median bootstrap CIs (Mann-Whitney robustness check); score-per-unit-time efficiency index.\\n6. Feasibility: version-pin JaxMARL, plan source checks (get_smax_maps map list, smax heuristic bot, ppo/iql config keys, qmix availability, whether smax is bundled), and in-silico sanity baselines (random-policy ~0, bot-vs-bot ~1) — these are PLANNED checks in the design, not executed experiments.\\n\\nPlease now produce the final experiment design deliverable implementing this agreed approach: full rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility. Explicitly distinguish fixed requirements (the study objective and JaxMARL/SMAX context) from our open choices (IQL vs QMIX, scenario selection, metric definitions, budgets), state all assumptions and unknowns, and clearly state that no experiment was run.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "9ef7775b-2bf6-4164-a4f5-6c2876870cd3", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:38:30.826686", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "8c9818bb-3535-48cd-837c-bb8d4042fe4b", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved the approach and now wants the final experiment design deliverable (stage=result). I have already gathered the evidence. Let me write the complete final design.\n\nThe deliverable should include:\n- Full rationale\n- Controls\n- Analysis plan\n- Feasibility\n- Distinguish fixed requirements from open choices\n- Assumptions and unknowns\n- Clear statement that no experiment was run\n\nLet me write a thorough, well-structured final design. I'll hand it off via end_researcher_turn with stage=result.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n1. Title / Study objective (fixed)\n2. What is fixed vs open (design decision table)\n3. Environment grounding (what was inspected)\n4. Design overview\n5. Algorithm specifics (IPPO, IQL, optional QMIX)\n6. Scenarios (3m, 8m, 5m_vs_6m)\n7. Metrics\n   - Training time (throughput, cumulative wall-clock, time-to-target)\n   - Normalized score (definition, secondary metrics)\n8. Controls (table)\n9. Evaluation protocol\n10. Analysis plan\n11. Feasibility (planned checks, not executed)\n12. Assumptions and unknowns\n13. Statement that no experiment was run\n\nI should be precise and cite the environment observations (224 CPUs, no GPU, no packages, no network, workspace contains only task.json, topics not present as files).\n\nLet me be careful about specifics of JaxMARL to avoid over-claiming:\n- IPPO: JaxMARL's PPO implementation - ppov2? Actually the jaxmarl/algorithms/ppo/ppov2.py has PPOV2 class. There's also an older ppo. For SMAX, the standard training script examples/smax/ uses PPOV2? In jaxmarl/examples there's smax example: examples/smax/ppo_smax.py and iql_smax.py. I recall examples: examples/smax/ppo_smax.py, examples/smax/iql_smax.py, examples/smax/qmix_smax.py. Yes I believe jaxmarl has examples/smax with ppo, iql, qmix files. I should mention this as source-supported but flag verify.\n\nActually let me recall: The JaxMARL repo examples folder has subfolders: examples/baselines (ppo, iql, qmix run scripts?), examples/smax (has ppo_smax.py, iql_smax.py, qmix_smax.py?). Yes, I'm fairly confident examples/smax exists with these scripts since the paper used SMAX. Also examples/hanabi, examples/rware etc.\n\nI'll mention these as expected source locations to verify, flagged as source-check items.\n\nAlso config: JaxMARL env configs in jaxmarl/environments/smax.py defines SMAXConfig with default parameters. The training script loads config dicts.\n\nTiming: The IQL uses a replay buffer (ReplayBuffer class in jaxmarl/algorithms/iql/replay_buffer.py). Updates use target network and epsilon-greedy. QMIX has mixing network and also replay.\n\nEval: win rate computed by running episodes and comparing reward (SMAX win condition: all enemy units dead → win). The smax env returns done when all units of a team destroyed, truncated at max steps.\n\nLet me include an a priori threshold and effect size plan.\n\nFeasibility checks (planned, not executed):\n- Install JaxMARL and confirm version tag; verify smax bundled or standalone.\n- Inspect get_smax_maps() to confirm map availability.\n- Inspect smax heuristic bot decision rule.\n- Inspect ppo/iql config keys and defaults; confirm qmix presence.\n- Sanity checks: random-policy win rate ~0; bot-vs-bot ~1; measure throughput pilot on 3m to size the budget.\n- Pilot run to sanity-check that each algorithm learns on 3m before full budget commitment.\n\nCompute estimate: rough feasibility note — CPU with 224 cores, 2 TB RAM. Example: 3m has ~9 units, small obs; 256 envs on CPU JAX should give thousands of env-steps/s; 2M steps → few hours per seed. But I must be careful not to claim actual measurements. I'll phrase as planning rationale for tractability with uncertainty.\n\nNote the analysis plan: curves mean±SEM across seeds; paired per-seed differences with bootstrap CIs; a priori meaningful-difference threshold (e.g., Δ ≥ 0.05 normalized score AND 95% CI excludes 0, plus effect size Cohen's d with |d| ≥ 0.5 as secondary). Time-to-target: bootstrap CI of median, Mann-Whitney robustness. Score-per-unit-time index = normalized score reached at budget / total wall-clock (or area under score-vs-time curve normalized). \n\nAlso discuss \"training time\" nuance: env-step parity vs wall-clock parity — we fix env-step budget, and measure wall-clock; since algorithms differ in per-step overhead, report both. \"What makes a difference meaningful\" — the threshold.\n\nAlso address the \"training efficiency\" from fixed objective: efficiency = normalized score achieved per unit wall-clock (and per env step).\n\nLet me now write the final deliverable. It should be self-contained. I'll put the full design in `answer`, the observations in `evidence`, and separate assumptions/uncertainties.\n\nI'll keep formatting clean.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Experiment Design: JaxMARL PPO (IPPO) vs Q-Learning (IQL) in SMAX — Training Time and Normalized Score\n\n## 0. Status declaration (fixed)\nThis is a **design-only deliverable**. **No experiment has been run and none is claimed.** All \"checks\" described below are planned verification and validation steps to be executed before/at run time; exploratory shell commands performed during design (listing the workspace, probing installed packages, CPU/RAM inspection) produced no experimental results.\n\n## 1. Fixed requirements vs. open choices\nFixed requirements (given by the task, not negotiable):\n- R1: Study objective — compare JaxMARL's PPO against a Q-Learning method in SMAX environments on (a) training efficiency/time and (b) normalized score (performance), within the JaxMARL/SMAX context.\n- R2: No experiment may be run or claimed; the deliverable is the design.\n- R3: Runtime context — CPU-only (no GPU), system Python, JaxMARL not preinstalled, network disabled; the design must be valid under these constraints.\n\nOpen choices (ours to define; each is labeled as OUR metric/design decision in the document):\n- C1: Which Q-Learning variant — IQL (primary) and optional QMIX (secondary, only if available).\n- C2: Scenario set — 3m, 8m, 5m_vs_6m.\n- C3: Definition of \"training time\" metrics.\n- C4: Definition of \"normalized score\".\n- C5: Controls (budgets, seeds, architecture, eval protocol, hyperparameter policy).\n- C6: Analysis plan and the meaning of a \"meaningful\" difference.\n\n## 2. Environment grounding (observed during design)\n- /workspace contains only task.json; no repository, no track.md file, no datasets, no local setup notes.\n- Network disabled; `jax`, `jaxmarl`, `smax` are not installed (import checks failed) → installation is a prerequisite at run time, and every API fact below is to be confirmed against the pinned source.\n- Hardware: 224 CPUs (`nproc`=224), ~2 TB RAM, GPU disabled.\n- The referenced setup topics (setup_2/4/12) are not available as files locally; grounding for SMAX/JaxMARL therefore rests on the public codebases pinned by version tag.\n\n## 3. Algorithms (open choice C1)\n- **IPPO (PPO arm):** JaxMARL's PPO implementation applied independently per agent: per-agent policy + value MLPs with shared observation encoder (implementation detail per JaxMARL's ppo config), GAE(λ) advantage estimation, clipped surrogate objective, minibatch updates, entropy regularization. Uses JaxMARL's SMAX-tuned default config (reported verbatim).\n- **IQL (Q-Learning arm, primary):** JaxMARL's Independent Q-Learning baseline — per-agent DQN-style Q-networks with target networks, prioritized/uniform replay buffer (JaxMARL's replay buffer), ε-greedy exploration with decay, periodic target update. Uses JaxMARL's default SMAX config verbatim.\n- **QMIX (secondary, conditional):** included ONLY if the pinned JaxMARL version ships the value-decomposition QMIX baseline (`jaxmarl/algorithms/qmix` present). Its availability, and its absence if not found, is stated explicitly in the run report. Rationale: QMIX tests whether centralized value decomposition changes the training-efficiency/performance conclusion vs. fully independent IQL; it is not required to answer the question.\n\n## 4. Scenarios (open choice C2) and rationale\n- **3m** (easy, symmetric, 3 Marauders vs 3), **8m** (medium, symmetric), **5m_vs_6m** (asymmetric, outnumbered) — spanning easy→medium and symmetric→asymmetric difficulty in SMAX's heuristic-bot setting, while staying CPU-tractable (small observation/action spaces, small unit counts). Rationale: difficulty span avoids cherry-picking one regime, and tractability protects the fixed-resource (CPU) validity requirement.\n- For each map the opponent is SMAX's built-in heuristic bot; the bot's decision rule is verified from source (planned check F2) before use.\n\n## 5. Training-time measurement (open choice C3; OUR metric definition)\nGoal: fair, machine-independent-as-possible comparison on CPU.\n- Environment parallelism fixed for BOTH arms: 256 parallel envs via JAX vmap, on a **pinned core count** (design value: 64 of 224; chosen to leave headroom and be reproducibly pinnable) on the same machine. The core count and JAX device config are fixed and reported.\n- Reported quantities:\n  1. **Throughput**: env-steps/s measured after a warmup period (e.g., after the first 10% of budget) so JIT-compilation effects are excluded; median over ≥3 repeated timing runs.\n  2. **Cumulative wall-clock** at fixed env-step checkpoints (e.g., every 10% of the per-map budget), enabling score-vs-time curves.\n  3. **Time-to-target**: wall-clock to first reach a target normalized score (see §6), per seed; compared across seeds.\n- Because IQL's replay/target-update loop has different per-step overhead than IPPO's on-policy loop, we explicitly distinguish (i) wall-clock per env-step (throughput) and (ii) total wall-clock to budget; both are reported — this is what makes \"training time\" meaningful rather than a single absolute number.\n\n## 6. Normalized-score definition (open choice C4; OUR metric definition)\n- Per-map primary metric: **normalized score = (wr − wr_rand)/(wr_ref − wr_rand)**, where:\n  - wr = evaluation win rate vs the built-in SMAX bot (deterministic/greedy eval, N ≥ 100 episodes),\n  - wr_rand = random-policy win rate, measured in silico (planned baseline check), anchor ≈ 0,\n  - wr_ref = reference win rate = heuristic bot vs itself, measured in silico, expected ≈ 1 (planned baseline check).\n- Rationale: normalizes away per-map difficulty and bot strength so scores are comparable across maps and the metric is anchored to the actual opponent; labeled explicitly in the report as our definition (the task does not pre-specify it).\n- Secondary metrics (reported alongside): raw win rate and mean episode return at the same checkpoints.\n\n## 7. Controls (open choice C5) — fixed for fair comparison\n| Dimension | Fixed value |\n|---|---|\n| Env-step budget per map-seed | 3m: 2M; 8m: 2M; 5m_vs_6m: 4M (revisable upward after feasibility pilot only) |\n| Network capacity | MLP [128,128], same activation, same obs/action encoding (SMAX wrapper defaults) applied to policy/value (IPPO) and Q (IQL); parameter count reported |\n| RL constants | Same discount γ, same max episode length, same reward shaping — SMAX defaults; greedy eval for both |\n| Hyperparameters | Each algorithm's JaxMARL default SMAX config, taken verbatim and reported; no per-algorithm tuning advantage; a separate optional tuning study is out of scope |\n| Parallelism/timing | 256 envs, core-pin 64, same machine, median of ≥3 timing runs |\n| Evaluation | Same cadence (every 5% of budget → every 100k steps at 2M budget), N ≥ 100 eval episodes, no exploration, same RNG scheme |\n| Seeds | ≥10 seeds (3m, 8m), ≥5 seeds (5m_vs_6m); identical RNG key management per algorithm |\n| Software | Single pinned JaxMARL version tag + fixed Python/Flax/Optax versions, recorded in the run report |\n\n## 8. Analysis plan (open choice C6)\n1. Per map: mean±SEM curves of normalized score vs env-step and vs wall-clock across seeds, IPPO vs IQL overlaid (QMIX if available).\n2. Final-score comparison: paired per-seed differences (IPPO − IQL) at the final checkpoint with bootstrap 95% CIs and effect sizes (Cohen's d). A priori \"meaningful difference\" rule: |Δ| ≥ 0.05 in normalized score AND bootstrap CI excluding 0 AND |d| ≥ 0.5 (secondary), all three together; otherwise \"comparable\".\n3. Training efficiency: normalized score achieved per unit wall-clock and per unit env-step; score-per-unit-time index = final normalized score / total wall-clock (median across seeds).\n4. Time-to-target: distributions across seeds with bootstrap CIs of the median; Mann–Whitney as a robustness check; reported per map and target level (e.g., target = 0.5 normalized).\n5. Tradeoff synthesis: score-vs-time overlay and the efficiency index, with an explicit statement of the regime (per map and per target level) in which each algorithm wins, if any.\n\n## 9. Feasibility (planned checks — design only, not executed)\n- F1: Install JaxMARL (pinned tag); record version; determine whether SMAX is bundled at jaxmarl/environments/smax or requires the standalone smax package; confirm examples (e.g., examples/smax) and algorithm entry points (algorithms/ppo, algorithms/iql, algorithms/qmix if present).\n- F2: Inspect source: get_smax_maps() for the actual map list (confirm 3m/8m/5m_vs_6m exist), the heuristic-bot rule, obs/action spec, reward/done/terminal semantics (win = all enemy units eliminated), and max episode length.\n- F3: Inspect PPO and IQL config keys/defaults for SMAX; record them verbatim for the controls table; record whether QMIX ships.\n- F4: In-silico sanity baselines: random-policy win rate ≈ 0 (validates normalized-score floor) and bot-vs-bot win rate ≈ 1 (validates wr_ref); both are validation runs of the metric definition, documented as such.\n- F5: Short pilot on 3m (both algorithms) to (a) confirm learning occurs, (b) measure real CPU throughput, (c) size the full budget and per-seed wall-clock before committing resources. This pilot is part of the execution plan, not claimed now.\n- Compute posture: 224 cores / ~2 TB RAM make the 256-env × 2–4M-step × 5–10-seed budget plausible on CPU; exact runtime is unknown until the pilot (listed under unknowns).\n\n## 10. Assumptions and unknowns\nAssumptions are stated in the dedicated field below. Key unknowns to resolve at run time: exact SMAX map list and bot rule; whether the pinned JaxMARL version ships QMIX; whether SMAX is bundled or separate; real CPU throughput and required budget per map (pilot); and acceptance of our normalized-score and training-time definitions as the study's metrics.\n\n## 11. Deliverables of the design when executed\nA per-map report containing: algorithm/config cards (verbatim configs), control table, training curves (score vs steps and vs wall-clock) with seed spread, normalized-score table with bootstrap CIs and effect sizes, time-to-target table, efficiency indices, feasibility/baseline-check results, and an explicit conclusions section applying the a priori meaningful-difference rule — all labeled with the caveat that they come from a to-be-run experiment.", "evidence": "Observed during design exploration (no experiment run): /workspace contains only task.json (which embeds the question, context, and a track.md note stating this is a design-only task; repositories/compute not required); network disabled; jax/jaxmarl/smax not installed (import checks fail); system Python only; 224 CPUs (nproc=224) and ~2 TB RAM; GPU disabled; no local copies of the referenced setup topics (setup_2/4/12) or any repo/source files exist, so API grounding is deferred to run-time version-pinned source inspection (planned checks F1–F3). Ha